<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno" lang="en">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">The Betti Numbers of a Determinantal Variety</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Mahir Bilen Can
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>Department of Mathematics
<br class="ltx_break">Tulane University
<br class="ltx_break">New Orleans, Louisiana 70118
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:mahirbilencan@gmail.com">mahirbilencan@gmail.com</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">Date: March 15, 2019</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We determine the Poincaré polynomial of the determinantal variety <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{\det=0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">det</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\det=0\}</annotation></semantics></math> in
the projective space associated with the monoid of <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> matrices.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Keywords:</span> Determinantal variety, Betti numbers, Chow groups, Borel-Moore homology 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="abstract1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MSC:</span> 14M12, 20M32, 14C15</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this note, we look closely at the homology groups of a classical variety.
Let <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the semigroup defined by the vanishing of the determinant polynomial in <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> matrices.
More precisely, we set <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{0}:=M\setminus G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{0}:=M\setminus G</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is the monoid of all linear operators on an <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> dimensional
complex vector space <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=\textrm{GL}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>GL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\textrm{GL}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The purpose of this note is to describe the Poincaré polynomial of the quotient,</p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Y:={\mathbb{P}}(Y^{0})=(Y^{0}\setminus\{0\})/{\mathbb{C}}^{*}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y:={\mathbb{P}}(Y^{0})=(Y^{0}\setminus\{0\})/{\mathbb{C}}^{*},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the center of <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{GL}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>GL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{GL}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is not difficult to see that if <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim V=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim V=2</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to the quadric surface <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{1}\times{\mathbb{P}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{1}\times{\mathbb{P}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, the Poincaré polynomial of <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is given by <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+2t^{2}+t^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+2t^{2}+t^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, in general, <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> has a large singular locus, which is given by
the projectivization of a <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\times G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\times G</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit closure, <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\overline{GeG}\setminus\{0\})/{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\overline{GeG}\setminus\{0\})/{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></span>,
where <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is an idempotent of rank <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-2</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is natural question to ask for a description of the Poincaré polynomial of <math id="S1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n:=\dim V&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n:=\dim V&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>.
It turns out that the degrees as well as the coefficients of monomials in <math id="S1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{Y}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{Y}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> have interesting patterns,
although <math id="S1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{Y}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{Y}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is neither symmetric nor unimodal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our main result and its corollary are the following statements.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.E2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_Theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.E2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.E2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.E2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.E2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.E2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> denote the determinantal variety defined as in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The Betti Numbers of a Determinantal Variety" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>).
If <math id="S1.E2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.E2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> dimensional, then the homology groups of <math id="S1.E2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the following isomorphisms:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}(Y)\cong\begin{cases}0&amp;\text{ if $k$ is odd and $k&lt;n^{2}-1$};\\
{\mathbb{Z}}&amp;\text{ if $k$ is even and $k&lt;n^{2}-1$};\\
H_{k+1-(n^{2}-1)}(\textrm{PSU}_{n})&amp;\text{ if $k$ is odd and $k\geq n^{2}-1$}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mi>k</mi><mtext> is odd and </mtext><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>ℤ</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mi>k</mi><mtext> is even and </mtext><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>PSU</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mi>k</mi><mtext> is odd and </mtext><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}(Y)\cong\begin{cases}0&amp;\text{ if $k$ is odd and $k&lt;n^{2}-1$};\\
{\mathbb{Z}}&amp;\text{ if $k$ is even and $k&lt;n^{2}-1$};\\
H_{k+1-(n^{2}-1)}(\textrm{PSU}_{n})&amp;\text{ if $k$ is odd and $k\geq n^{2}-1$}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.E2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Finally, if <math id="S1.E2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is even and <math id="S1.E2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq n^{2}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq n^{2}-1</annotation></semantics></math>, then we have
<span id="S1.E2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S1.E2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}(Y)/H_{k+1-(n^{2}-1)}(\textrm{PSU}_{n})\cong{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>PSU</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}(Y)/H_{k+1-(n^{2}-1)}(\textrm{PSU}_{n})\cong{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
Here, <math id="S1.E2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{PSU}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>PSU</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{PSU}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the projective special unitary group.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let us denote by <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{PSU}_{n}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mtext>PSU</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{PSU}_{n}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> the polynomial <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{i=1}^{n-1}(1+t^{2i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{i=1}^{n-1}(1+t^{2i+1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
In other words, <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{PSU}_{n}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mtext>PSU</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{PSU}_{n}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Poincaré polynomial of <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{PSU}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>PSU</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{PSU}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is easy to check that, starting from <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5</annotation></semantics></math> the polynomial <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{PSU}_{n}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mtext>PSU</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{PSU}_{n}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is no longer unimodal.
On the other hand, as a product of palindromic polynomials,
<math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{PSU}_{n}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mtext>PSU</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{PSU}_{n}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is palindromic for all <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let us write <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{PSU}_{n}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mtext>PSU</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{PSU}_{n}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> in the form <math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{PSU}_{n}}(t)=\sum_{i=0}^{n^{2}-1}b_{i}t^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mtext>PSU</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{PSU}_{n}}(t)=\sum_{i=0}^{n^{2}-1}b_{i}t^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.E3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_Corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.E3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.E3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.E3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.E3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.E3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> denote the determinantal variety defined as in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The Betti Numbers of a Determinantal Variety" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>).
If <math id="S1.E3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.E3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> dimensional, then the Poincaré polynomial of <math id="S1.E3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is expressible as a sum of two polynomials,</p>
<table id="S1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{Y}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{Y}(t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=A(t)+\widetilde{B}(t)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=A(t)+\widetilde{B}(t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.E3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.E3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(t)=1+t^{2}+\cdots+t^{2\lfloor\frac{n^{2}-1}{2}\rfloor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(t)=1+t^{2}+\cdots+t^{2\lfloor\frac{n^{2}-1}{2}\rfloor}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.E3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{B}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{B}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is the polynomial that is obtained from</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(t):=t^{n^{2}-1}P_{\textrm{PSU}_{n}}(t)=\sum_{i=n^{2}-1}^{2(n^{2}-1)}b_{i-(n^{2}-1)}t^{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mtext>PSU</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(t):=t^{n^{2}-1}P_{\textrm{PSU}_{n}}(t)=\sum_{i=n^{2}-1}^{2(n^{2}-1)}b_{i-(n^{2}-1)}t^{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.E3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">by adding 1 to the coefficients of the terms <math id="S1.E3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i-(n^{2}-1)}t^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i-(n^{2}-1)}t^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.E3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> odd.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Note that a complete description of the (torsion in the) cohomology ring of <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{PSU}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>PSU</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{PSU}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> has recently been given
by Haibao Duan in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start with reviewing some well known facts about the Chow groups and Borel-Moore homology
groups. We follow the presentation in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Chapter 19]</cite>; if <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a topological space,
then <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{H}_{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{H}_{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> stands for the Borel-Moore homology group with integer coefficients.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> be a nonnegative integer, and let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a scheme.
The free abelian group generated by all <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> dimensional
subvarieties of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{k}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{k}X</annotation></semantics></math>. The elements of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{k}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{k}X</annotation></semantics></math>
are called <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-cycles.
A <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-cycle <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be rationally equivalent to 0,
and written <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\sim 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∼</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\sim 0</annotation></semantics></math> if there are a finite number of
<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> dimensional subvarieties <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{1},\dots,Y_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1},\dots,Y_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and
rational functions
<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}\in{\mathbb{C}}(Y_{i})^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}\in{\mathbb{C}}(Y_{i})^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,s</annotation></semantics></math>)</span>
such that
<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\sum[\text{div}(f_{i})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mtext>div</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\sum[\text{div}(f_{i})]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The set of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-cycles which are rationally equivalent to 0 is
a subgroup of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Rat_{k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Rat_{k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The quotient group
<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}(X):=Z_{k}(X)/Rat_{k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}(X):=Z_{k}(X)/Rat_{k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>
is called the <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">group of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-cycles modulo rational equivalence</span>, or
the <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Chow group</span>.
The total Chow group <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{*}(X):=\bigoplus_{k=0}^{\dim X}A_{k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{*}(X):=\bigoplus_{k=0}^{\dim X}A_{k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is a graded
abelian group; if <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is equidimensional, then <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\dim X}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\dim X}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is freely generated by
the classes of irreducible components of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is an equidimensional scheme, by replacing <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, that is the group of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-codimensional cycles,
we have the Chow group</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{k}(X):=Z^{k}(X)/Rat_{\dim X-k}(X)=A_{\dim X-k}(X)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{k}(X):=Z^{k}(X)/Rat_{\dim X-k}(X)=A_{\dim X-k}(X).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We set <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X):=\oplus A^{i}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X):=\oplus A^{i}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, then there is an intersection pairing on <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>,
and hence, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> becomes a ring.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> be an element of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}\cup\{*\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}\cup\{*\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We will denote the vector spaces <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{s}(X)\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{s}(X)\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{s}(X)\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{s}(X)\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{s}(X)_{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{s}(X)_{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{s}(X)_{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{s}(X)_{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Chow groups behave nicely with respect to certain classes of morphisms.</p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper morphism,
then there is a (covariant) homomorphism</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{*}:A_{k}(X)\rightarrow A_{k}(Y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{*}:A_{k}(X)\rightarrow A_{k}(Y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> is flat morphism of relative dimension <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>,
then there is a (contravariant) homomorphism</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}:A_{k}(Y)\rightarrow A_{k+n}(X)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}:A_{k}(Y)\rightarrow A_{k+n}(X).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i:Y\hookrightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i:Y\hookrightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> be an inclusion of a closed subscheme <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>
into a scheme <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> denote the complement <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X-Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X-Y</annotation></semantics></math>
and let <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:U\rightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:U\rightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> denote the inclusion.
Then there is an exact sequence</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{k}(Y)\stackrel{{\scriptstyle i_{*}}}{{\longrightarrow}}A_{k}(X)\stackrel{{\scriptstyle j^{*}}}{{\longrightarrow}}A_{k}(U)\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mover><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mover><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{k}(Y)\stackrel{{\scriptstyle i_{*}}}{{\longrightarrow}}A_{k}(X)\stackrel{{\scriptstyle j^{*}}}{{\longrightarrow}}A_{k}(U)\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">for all <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. To understand the image of <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>
we need to consider Edidin and Graham’s version of
Bloch’s higher Chow groups.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a quasi-projective scheme, and
let <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the algebraic version of the regular <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-simplex:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{k}=\text{Spec}({\mathbb{Z}}[t_{1},\dots,t_{k}]/(t_{1}+\cdots+t_{k}-1))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Spec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{k}=\text{Spec}({\mathbb{Z}}[t_{1},\dots,t_{k}]/(t_{1}+\cdots+t_{k}-1)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S2.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">face</span> of <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the subscheme of the form
<math id="S2.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times\Delta^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times\Delta^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the second factor <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is the image
of an injective canonical morphism <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:\Delta^{m}\rightarrow\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:\Delta^{m}\rightarrow\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We denote by <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{i}(X,\bullet)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{i}(X,\bullet)</annotation></semantics></math> the complex
whose <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th term is the group of cycles of codimension
<math id="S2.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> which intersect properly all of the faces
in <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, Bloch considered the following higher Chow groups:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CH^{i}(X,m):=H_{m}(Z^{i}(X,\bullet))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CH^{i}(X,m):=H_{m}(Z^{i}(X,\bullet)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{p}(X,\bullet)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{p}(X,\bullet)</annotation></semantics></math> denote
the complex
whose <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th term is the group of cycles of dimension
<math id="S2.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+k</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> intersecting the faces properly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_Definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.E2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S2.E2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,k)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.E2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-th higher Chow group</span>
of a quasi-projective scheme <math id="S2.E2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{p}(X,k):=H_{k}(Z_{p}(X,\bullet))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{p}(X,k):=H_{k}(Z_{p}(X,\bullet)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The point of this definition is that <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> does not need to be equidimensional.
If <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is equidimensional of dimension <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, then it is easy to see that
<math id="S2.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{p}(X,k)=CH^{n-p}(X,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{p}(X,k)=CH^{n-p}(X,k)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Now we state the localization long exact sequence for
higher Chow groups.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_Lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.E4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.E4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed, not necessarily equidimensional subscheme of
an equidimensional scheme <math id="S2.E4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then there is a long exact sequence of higher Chow groups;</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\cdots\rightarrow A_{p}(Y,k)\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\cdots\rightarrow A_{p}(Y,k)\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{p}(X,k)\rightarrow A_{p}(X-Y,k)\rightarrow\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{p}(X,k)\rightarrow A_{p}(X-Y,k)\rightarrow\cdots</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\cdots\rightarrow A_{p}(X-Y,1)\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\cdots\rightarrow A_{p}(X-Y,1)\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{p}(Y)\rightarrow A_{p}(X)\rightarrow A_{p}(X-Y)\rightarrow 0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{p}(Y)\rightarrow A_{p}(X)\rightarrow A_{p}(X-Y)\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, Lemma 4]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="ThmRemark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_Remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="ThmRemark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="ThmRemark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="ThmRemark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="ThmRemark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is not clear if the localization long exact sequence terminates for an arbitrary scheme.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Borel-Moore homology groups of a space are defined by using
ordinary cohomology groups as follows. Let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a topological space that is embedded as a
closed subspace of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for some positive integer <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>th Borel-Moore homology
of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{H}_{q}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{H}_{q}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{H}_{q}(Y)=H^{n-q}({\mathbb{R}}^{n},{\mathbb{R}}^{n}\setminus Y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{H}_{q}(Y)=H^{n-q}({\mathbb{R}}^{n},{\mathbb{R}}^{n}\setminus Y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:Y\rightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:Y\rightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper morphism of complex schemes,
then there are covariant homomorphisms <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{*}:\bar{H}_{i}(Y)\rightarrow\bar{H}_{i}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{*}:\bar{H}_{i}(Y)\rightarrow\bar{H}_{i}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:U\hookrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:U\hookrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> is an open imbedding, then
there are contravariant restriction homomorphisms <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{*}:\bar{H}_{i}(Y)\rightarrow\bar{H}_{i}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{*}:\bar{H}_{i}(Y)\rightarrow\bar{H}_{i}(U)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is the complement of <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i:Y\rightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i:Y\rightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> is the
closed imbedding, then there is a long exact sequence</p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\cdots\rightarrow\bar{H}_{i+1}(U)\rightarrow\bar{H}_{i}(Y)\overset{i_{*}}{\longrightarrow}\bar{H}_{i}(X)\overset{j^{*}}{\longrightarrow}\bar{H}_{i}(U)\rightarrow\bar{H}_{i-1}(Y)\rightarrow\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msub></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msup></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\cdots\rightarrow\bar{H}_{i+1}(U)\rightarrow\bar{H}_{i}(Y)\overset{i_{*}}{\longrightarrow}\bar{H}_{i}(X)\overset{j^{*}}{\longrightarrow}\bar{H}_{i}(U)\rightarrow\bar{H}_{i-1}(Y)\rightarrow\cdots</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a disjoint union of a finite number of spaces, <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=X_{1}\cup\cdots\cup X_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=X_{1}\cup\cdots\cup X_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
then <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{H}_{i}(X)=\oplus\bar{H}_{i}(X_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{H}_{i}(X)=\oplus\bar{H}_{i}(X_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is a Künneth formula for Borel-Moore homology.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(6)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.I2.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S2.I2.i6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional complex scheme,
then <math id="S2.I2.i6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{H}_{i}(X)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{H}_{i}(X)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.I2.i6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;2n</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.I2.i6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{H}_{2n}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{H}_{2n}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is a free abelian group
with one generator for each irreducible component <math id="S2.I2.i6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.I2.i6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
The generator corresponding to <math id="S2.I2.i6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> will be denoted by <math id="S2.I2.i6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cl(X_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cl(X_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
More generally, we will use the following notation: If <math id="S2.I2.i6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is a
<math id="S2.I2.i6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional closed subscheme
of <math id="S2.I2.i6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.I2.i6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i:V\hookrightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i:V\hookrightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> is the closed imbedding, then
<math id="S2.I2.i6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cl_{X}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cl_{X}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> stands for <math id="S2.I2.i6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{*}cl(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{*}cl(V)</annotation></semantics></math>, which lives in <math id="S2.I2.i6.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{H}_{2k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{H}_{2k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.
If confusion is unlikely, we will omit the subscript <math id="S2.I2.i6.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> from the notation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(7)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.I2.i7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:V\rightarrow W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:V\rightarrow W</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper, surjective morphism of varieties, then
<math id="S2.I2.i7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{*}cl(V)=\text{deg}(V/W)\cdot cl(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>deg</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{*}cl(V)=\text{deg}(V/W)\cdot cl(W)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since we do not need it for our purposes,
we will not define <math id="S2.I2.i7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{deg}(V/W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>deg</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{deg}(V/W)</annotation></semantics></math> here; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Section 1.4]</cite> for its definition.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(8)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any complex scheme <math id="S2.I2.i8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
there is a homomorphism from algebraic <math id="S2.I2.i8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-cycles on <math id="S2.I2.i8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
to the <math id="S2.I2.i8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th Borel-Moore homology,
<math id="S2.I2.i8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cl:Z_{k}(X)\rightarrow\bar{H}_{2k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cl:Z_{k}(X)\rightarrow\bar{H}_{2k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, defined
by <math id="S2.I2.i8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cl(\sum n_{i}[V_{i}])=\sum n_{i}cl_{X}(V_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cl(\sum n_{i}[V_{i}])=\sum n_{i}cl_{X}(V_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
This homomorphism factors through the “algebraic equivalence”
(which we didn’t introduce), hence, by composition,
it induces a homomorphism from the <math id="S2.I2.i8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th Chow group
of <math id="S2.I2.i8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> onto the <math id="S2.I2.i8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math>-th Borel-Moore homology.
We will denote the resulting homomorphism by <math id="S2.I2.i8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cl" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cl</annotation></semantics></math> also,
and call it the <span id="S2.I2.i8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cycle map</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i9" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(9)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If a complex scheme <math id="S2.I2.i9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has a cellular decomposition, then the cycle map
<math id="S2.I2.i9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cl_{X}:A_{k}(X)\rightarrow\bar{H}_{2k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cl_{X}:A_{k}(X)\rightarrow\bar{H}_{2k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>
is an isomorphism (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Section 19.1.11]</cite>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i10" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(10)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, let us mention that if <math id="S2.I2.i10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S2.I2.i10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional
oriented manifold, then <math id="S2.I2.i10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{H}_{k}(X)\cong H^{n-k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{H}_{k}(X)\cong H^{n-k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For further details of this useful homology theory, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Proof</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will use the following notation in the sequel:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{lcl}M&amp;:&amp;\text{ the monoid of $n\times n$ matrices defined over ${\mathbb{C}}$;}\\
G&amp;:&amp;\text{ the general linear group of $n\times n$ matrices defined over ${\mathbb{C}}$;}\\
T&amp;:&amp;\text{ the maximal torus consisting of diagonal matrices in $G$;}\\
Z&amp;:&amp;\text{ the center of $G$;}\\
X&amp;:&amp;\text{ the projectivization of $M$, $X:=(M\setminus\{0\})/Z$;}\\
Y_{0}&amp;:&amp;\text{ the vanishing locus of the determinant in $M$;}\\
Y&amp;:&amp;\text{ the projectivization of $Y$, $Y:=Y^{0}/Z$;}\\
U&amp;:&amp;\text{ the projectivization of $G$, $U:=G/Z=\textrm{PGL}_{n}$.}\\
\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>M</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>:</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext> the monoid of </mtext><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mtext> matrices defined over </mtext><mi>ℂ</mi><mtext>;</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>G</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>:</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext> the general linear group of </mtext><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mtext> matrices defined over </mtext><mi>ℂ</mi><mtext>;</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>T</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>:</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext> the maximal torus consisting of diagonal matrices in </mtext><mi>G</mi><mtext>;</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>Z</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>:</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext> the center of </mtext><mi>G</mi><mtext>;</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>X</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>:</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext> the projectivization of </mtext><mi>M</mi><mtext>, </mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><mtext>;</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>:</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext> the vanishing locus of the determinant in </mtext><mi>M</mi><mtext>;</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>Y</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>:</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext> the projectivization of </mtext><mi>Y</mi><mtext>, </mtext><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><mtext>;</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>U</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>:</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext> the projectivization of </mtext><mi>G</mi><mtext>, </mtext><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mtext>PGL</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{lcl}M&amp;:&amp;\text{ the monoid of $n\times n$ matrices defined over ${\mathbb{C}}$;}\\
G&amp;:&amp;\text{ the general linear group of $n\times n$ matrices defined over ${\mathbb{C}}$;}\\
T&amp;:&amp;\text{ the maximal torus consisting of diagonal matrices in $G$;}\\
Z&amp;:&amp;\text{ the center of $G$;}\\
X&amp;:&amp;\text{ the projectivization of $M$, $X:=(M\setminus\{0\})/Z$;}\\
Y_{0}&amp;:&amp;\text{ the vanishing locus of the determinant in $M$;}\\
Y&amp;:&amp;\text{ the projectivization of $Y$, $Y:=Y^{0}/Z$;}\\
U&amp;:&amp;\text{ the projectivization of $G$, $U:=G/Z=\textrm{PGL}_{n}$.}\\
\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a projective variety, we have <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{H}_{q}(Y)=H_{q}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{H}_{q}(Y)=H_{q}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\{0,\dots,\dim Y\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\{0,\dots,\dim Y\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Of course, similar equalities hold true for <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{n^{2}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{n^{2}-1}</annotation></semantics></math> as well.
Both of the spaces <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> are path connected, therefore, we have <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}(X)=H_{0}(Y)={\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}(X)=H_{0}(Y)={\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The complement of <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the group <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is open in <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a long exact sequence for their Borel-Moore homology,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dots\to\bar{H}_{q}(Y)\to\bar{H}_{q}(X)\to\bar{H}_{q}(U)\to\bar{H}_{q-1}(Y)\to\dots," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dots\to\bar{H}_{q}(Y)\to\bar{H}_{q}(X)\to\bar{H}_{q}(U)\to\bar{H}_{q-1}(Y)\to\dots,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">As complex projective spaces have zero odd homology,
the long exact sequence in (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3. Proof ‣ The Betti Numbers of a Determinantal Variety" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) breaks up into short exact sequences.
More precisely, for <math id="S3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=1,\dots,n^{2}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=1,\dots,n^{2}-1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0\to\bar{H}_{2q+1}(U)\to{H}_{2q}(Y)\to H_{2q}(X)\to\bar{H}_{2q}(U)\to H_{2q-1}(Y)\to 0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0\to\bar{H}_{2q+1}(U)\to{H}_{2q}(Y)\to H_{2q}(X)\to\bar{H}_{2q}(U)\to H_{2q-1}(Y)\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We will identify <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=\textrm{PGL}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mtext>PGL</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\textrm{PGL}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with the (complex) projective special linear group, <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{PSL}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>PSL</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{PSL}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In turn, as a real manifold, <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{PSL}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>PSL</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{PSL}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> has the (Cartan-Malcev-Iwasawa) decomposition
<math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{PSL}_{n}\cong\textrm{PSU}_{n}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>PSL</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>PSU</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{PSL}_{n}\cong\textrm{PSU}_{n}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{PSU}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>PSU</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{PSU}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the projective
special unitary group, and <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=n^{2}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=n^{2}-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that, as a (real) Lie group, <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{PGL}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>PGL</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{PGL}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is an oriented <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2(n^{2}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2(n^{2}-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional manifold,
therefore, its Borel-Moore homology groups are actually cohomology groups,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{H}_{q}(U)=H^{2(n^{2}-1)-q}(U)=H^{2(n^{2}-1)-q}(\textrm{PSL}_{n})=H^{2(n^{2}-1)-q}(\textrm{PSU}_{n})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>PSL</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>PSU</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{H}_{q}(U)=H^{2(n^{2}-1)-q}(U)=H^{2(n^{2}-1)-q}(\textrm{PSL}_{n})=H^{2(n^{2}-1)-q}(\textrm{PSU}_{n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The unitary groups are compact.
Since <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{PSU}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>PSU</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{PSU}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n^{2}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n^{2}-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold, by Poincaré duality, we see the following fact.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_Lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.E3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The homology groups of <math id="S3.E3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=\textrm{PGL}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mtext>PGL</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\textrm{PGL}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are given by</p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{H}_{q}(U)=\begin{cases}0&amp;\text{ if }q&lt;n^{2}-1,\\
H_{q-(n^{2}-1)}(\textrm{PSU}_{n})&amp;\text{ if }q\geq n^{2}-1.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>PSU</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{H}_{q}(U)=\begin{cases}0&amp;\text{ if }q&lt;n^{2}-1,\\
H_{q-(n^{2}-1)}(\textrm{PSU}_{n})&amp;\text{ if }q\geq n^{2}-1.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By using (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3. Proof ‣ The Betti Numbers of a Determinantal Variety" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>) and the short exact sequence in (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3. Proof ‣ The Betti Numbers of a Determinantal Variety" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>),
we determine the homology groups of <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> in lower degrees.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_Lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.E5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The homology groups <math id="S3.E5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{q}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{q}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.E5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&lt;n^{2}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&lt;n^{2}-1</annotation></semantics></math> are given by</p>
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H_{q}(Y)=\begin{cases}0&amp;\text{ if $q$ is odd and $q&lt;n^{2}-1$},\\
{\mathbb{Z}}&amp;\text{ if $q$ is even and $q&lt;n^{2}-1$}.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mi>q</mi><mtext> is odd and </mtext><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>ℤ</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mi>q</mi><mtext> is even and </mtext><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{q}(Y)=\begin{cases}0&amp;\text{ if $q$ is odd and $q&lt;n^{2}-1$},\\
{\mathbb{Z}}&amp;\text{ if $q$ is even and $q&lt;n^{2}-1$}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="ThmRemark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_Remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="ThmRemark2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2</span></span><span id="ThmRemark2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="ThmRemark2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="ThmRemark2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="ThmRemark2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is an irreducible hypersurface in <math id="ThmRemark2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
the knowledge of the lower degree homology groups as in (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Lemma 3.5. ‣ 3. Proof ‣ The Betti Numbers of a Determinantal Variety" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>)
could also be obtained by using the Lefschetz hyperplane theorem,
see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Corollary 1.24]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We are now ready to state and prove our main result that is stated in the introduction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.E2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Betti Numbers of a Determinantal Variety" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\{1,\dots,n^{2}-2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\{1,\dots,n^{2}-2\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the commuting diagram of Chow groups and Betti numbers as in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 3.1 ‣ Proof of Theorem . ‣ 3. Proof ‣ The Betti Numbers of a Determinantal Variety" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="114.82" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 646.06 114.82" width="646.06"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,114.82) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(362.41,0) translate(0,51.15)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -69.11 36.16)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.88em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="39.8"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.F1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{q}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{q}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -186.5 36.16)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.77em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="38.36"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.F1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{q}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{q}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 49.79 36.16)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.76em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="38.21"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.F1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{q}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{q}(U)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 144.18 34.91)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.F1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -70.14 -42.58)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.03em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="41.86"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.F1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{H}_{2q}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{H}_{2q}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -187.53 -42.58)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.92em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="40.42"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.F1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{H}_{2q}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{H}_{2q}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -302.73 -42.58)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.92em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="54.27"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.F1.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{H}_{2q+1}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{H}_{2q+1}(U)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -307.46 36.16)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.61em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="63.75"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.F1.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{q+1}(U,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{q+1}(U,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 48.77 -42.58)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.91em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="40.26"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.F1.pic1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{H}_{2q}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{H}_{2q}(U)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><